Z||000000||88GT3||05-27-2000||11:16 AM||||There is an 88GT in the paper here.  It has 177K miles and they are asking $2200.  The sail panels are cracked. I haven't seen it yet, but he says the paint is in good shape except a key scratch on the right side.  A few thousand miles ago the check engine soon light came on and then went off.  Car has run fine since, but when it went to be smogged it failed because of the code stored in the computer.  At least that is what he says.  <P>What do you guys think?||||reg||1
Z||000001||William Federle||05-27-2000||11:27 AM||||I think that you could get him to let you retrieve the code from the computer. If what you find doesn't worry you too much, drive it and if you like it and if the car doesn't smoke or leak oil and the scratch isn't too bad and the ball joints and other greasable things don't look torn and the upolstry isn't ripped and everything works then offer him 1200 - 1500. Be prepared to put in all new sensors.<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 05-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||phillip||05-27-2000||12:52 PM||||is that "change all the sensors" thing common when getting a fiero. i'm trying to find a starting place with the '84 i just got. what kind of fundage are we talking? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||05-27-2000||01:22 PM||||I've heard that it is and that's what I ended up doing (a little at a time). I just want to advise you that I'm just learning as I try to keep my car on the road so bear that in mind. I replaced my throttle position sensor - about $35.00 at Advance auto (parts america). The car drove better with the new one. I replaced the manifold air pressure sensor (clipped to the r.h. side of the air cleaner assy)in my campaign to reduce my idle speed. It didn't change anything. It cost about $25.00. I changed the water temp sensor (the one plugged into the thermostat neck - it goes to the ECM). It didn't dramatically affect the idle but the car's "drivability" seemed to improve. I replaced the O2 sensor since I would get intermittant Check Engine Light activations and the computer would store a code 13 (I think). That never happened again after I put the new O2 sensor in. The temperature sensor cost about $18.00 and the O2 sensor cost about $25.00. (most of the stuff came from autozone.) I replaced the water temp sender (it runs the temp gage). The new one gave the same readings as the old one. It costs about $25.00. I got a mechanical temp gage and screwed it in where the fan switch normally goes (LH end of head pointed toward front of car). It cost about $10.00 and I mounted the gage under the dash where I can see it. It showed that the first mark (1/4 full scale) on the temp gage isn't 160 deg as I thought but 190 deg. I replaced the Idle Air Control Valve for about $25.00 and that seemed to help the idle problem (but didn't completely solve it.<P>This stuff might cost a different amount for a 1988 Fiero. <P>I'm sorry to be so long winded. The one thing I'd do different if I had it to do over is that I'd check the suspension components that get greased. Many of them on my car have torn seals - they won't hold grease. I'm eventually going to have them all replaced, but it's a lot of work that I would have avoided if I had thought of it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 05-27-2000).]||||reg||1
